    "auto-carriage-return": "A line feed also returns the head to the left margin (switch 1-2)",
    "graphics-table": "Codes 128-254 from IBM character set #2 rather than italics (switch 1-7 off)",
    "auto-line-feed": "Each carriage return also feeds a line (switch 1-8 off)",
    "download-characters": "The RAM holds download characters rather than a 4K input buffer (switch 2-1 off)",
    "character-set": "Power-on international set: any of the thirteen `ESC R` sets, by name or number (switches 2-2 to 2-4 give 0-7)",
    "paper-width": "Paper width in inches, tear-off strips included; the line is centred on it",
    "top-offset": "Inches from the perforation to the top of the first line",
    "stationery": "The paper: `fanfold` (plain, with tear-off strips), `sheet` (no strips), `green-bar`, `blue-bar`, `music-ruled`",
    "font": "The pica draft characters: an installed font's name, or the path of a dot font file",
    "elite-font": "The elite draft characters: an installed font's name, or the path of a dot font file",
}, defaults={"paper-width": "`9.5`, or `8.5` for `sheet`"}) }}

Star's additions to ESC/P are obeyed: absolute and relative positioning
(`ESC $`, `ESC \`), both margins at once (`ESC X`), extra space between
characters (`ESC SP`), moving to a column (`ESC f 0`), the slashed zero
(`ESC ~`), overline (`ESC _`), the top margin (`ESC c`), reverse line
feed and top of page (`ESC LF`, `ESC FF`) and the macro (`ESC +`).
Character sizes print as the LC-10 prints them, always in near letter
quality: double and quadruple size and their halves (`ESC h`), double
height on the base line (`ESC w`), and `ESC [ @`.

Download characters are defined as the LC-10 defines them, in draft or,
with NLQ selected, in NLQ's two-pass grid (`ESC &`, `ESC %`, `ESC :`).

Not modelled yet: IBM mode (switch 1-6).

